  <h1>What Is POCT?</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> POCT (point-of-care testing) is diagnostic testing performed near the patient — at the bedside, in a clinic, pharmacy or field — rather than in a central laboratory. It delivers results in minutes, enabling immediate clinical or operational decisions.</p>

  <h2>What defines POCT</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Proximity</strong> — testing where the patient is, not where the lab is.</li>
    <li><strong>Speed</strong> — results in minutes, not hours or days.</li>
    <li><strong>Simplicity</strong> — instrument-free or minimal-instrument operation.</li>
    <li><strong>Sample</strong> — whole blood, swab, urine or saliva at the point of collection.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where POCT is used</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Emergency and triage</strong> — cardiac markers (troponin), blood gas.</li>
    <li><strong>Infectious disease</strong> — dengue, malaria, influenza, COVID, HIV screening.</li>
    <li><strong>Primary care and pharmacy</strong> — Strep A, pregnancy, drug screening.</li>
    <li><strong>Field and outbreak settings</strong> — instrument-free rapid tests.</li>
    <li><strong>Occupational health</strong> — workplace drug testing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Advantages</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Faster time to diagnosis and treatment</li>
    <li>Decentralized — reaches settings without lab infrastructure</li>
    <li>Low-skill, minimal-equipment operation</li>
    <li>Potential room-temperature storage, depending on formulation</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sensitivity may be lower than laboratory methods</li>
    <li>Visual results can be subjective</li>
    <li>Requires operator training and QC for reliable use</li>
    <li>Results are screening-grade — confirmatory testing may be needed</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>What is the difference between POCT and IVD?</strong> IVD is the broad category; POCT is the subset performed near the patient.</p>
    <p><strong>Are all rapid tests POCT?</strong> Most are — a rapid test run near the patient is POCT.</p>
    <p><strong>Is POCT as accurate as lab testing?</strong> It depends on the analyte and assay; confirmatory lab testing may be required for critical results.</p>
  </div>

